---
/**
 * Scarcity — Economics-lens concept at Single scale.
 * Relative finitude of degrees of freedom and causal options, weighted by Mass.
 * Keep inline tags on one line with outer spaces (Astro whitespace).
 * No em-dashes in public copy.
 */
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Scarcity | Identity Engineering">
	<main class="min-h-screen">
		<section class="min-h-[50vh] flex flex-col justify-center pt-28 pb-16 border-b border-white/10">
			<div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-8">
				<div class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[4px] text-white/40 mb-6">
					Economics lens · Single Identity
				</div>
				<h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl font-bold tracking-tighter leading-[0.95] mb-6 text-white">
					Scarcity
				</h1>
				<p class="text-xl text-white/50 font-light leading-relaxed mb-6">
					Relative finitude of degrees of freedom and causal options, weighted by Mass.
					What makes allocation and value possible under the Metric Stem.
				</p>
				<div class="rounded-2xl border border-amber-500/20 bg-amber-500/[0.04] p-4">
					<p class="text-sm text-white/65 leading-relaxed">
						<strong class="text-white/80">Status:</strong> Core concept under the Economics lens.
						Starting point of the Single scale. Analogy, not isomorphism.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="px-8 py-16 border-b border-white/10">
			<div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-6 text-white/60 leading-relaxed">
				<div class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[4px] text-white/40">Definition</div>
				<h2 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white">Scarcity relative to Mass.</h2>
				<p>
					<strong class="text-white/85">Scarcity</strong> is the relative finitude of degrees of freedom and future causal options as measured by the Metric Stem and weighted by Mass.
					It is not an absolute inventory of objects. It is the geometric condition that some paths remain costly or closed while others remain open.
				</p>
				<p>
					Value arises where scarcity and Mass meet.
					Scarcity without Mass is noise. Mass without scarcity loses economic pressure.
					Together they create the tension that forces prioritization under the Vision Gradient.
				</p>
				<p>
					This reading stays valid whether the scarce thing is framed as a classical resource, as jurisdiction over compute, or as access to another Identity.
					The ontological label is secondary. The degree of scarcity relative to Mass is primary.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="px-8 py-16 border-b border-white/10 bg-white/[0.02]">
			<div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-8">
				<div class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[4px] text-white/40">What Scarcity is</div>
				<ul class="space-y-5 text-lg text-white/60 leading-relaxed">
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/85">Relative finitude:</strong> degrees of freedom and causal options are limited under a given Metric Stem.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/85">Weighted by Mass:</strong> scarcity only generates economic pressure where there is also geometric weight and resistance.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/85">Driver of prioritization:</strong> choice under scarcity reveals what the Stem actually values.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/85">Precondition of Preference and Opportunity:</strong> without scarcity, rankings lose force and opportunity cost becomes empty.
					</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="px-8 py-16 border-b border-white/10">
			<div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-8">
				<div class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[4px] text-white/40">What Scarcity is not</div>
				<ul class="space-y-5 text-lg text-white/60 leading-relaxed">
					<li class="border-l border-rose-400/30 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/85">Not absolute poverty.</strong>
						An Identity can hold high Mass and still face binding scarcity in specific domains (attention, coherent context, future optionality).
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-rose-400/30 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/85">Not a fixed list of objects.</strong>
						What is scarce is frame-relative and can shift with the Metric Stem and with changes in Mass.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-rose-400/30 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/85">Not solved by declaring abundance.</strong>
						Preference, Ownership and Opportunity still operate under real limits of degrees of freedom.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-rose-400/30 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/85">Not identical with the absence of Intelligence.</strong>
						Intelligence can expand options while specific high-Mass degrees of freedom remain scarce.
					</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="px-8 py-16 border-b border-white/10 bg-white/[0.02]">
			<div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-6 text-white/60 leading-relaxed">
				<div class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[4px] text-white/40">Engineering</div>
				<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">What we work on</h2>
				<ul class="space-y-5 text-lg">
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/85">Map binding scarcities:</strong> which degrees of freedom are actually limited under the current Metric Stem and Mass profile.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/85">Separate noise from pressure:</strong> scarcity without Mass does not drive real allocation; focus on the intersection.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/85">Align allocation with the Vision Gradient</strong> so that scarce high-Mass degrees of freedom reinforce the Stem rather than dilute it.
					</li>
					<li class="border-l border-white/15 pl-5">
						<strong class="text-white/85">Watch for false abundance:</strong> more Intelligence or more compute does not automatically remove scarcity of coherent attention or future optionality.
					</li>
				</ul>

				<div class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-black/30 p-6">
					<div class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[3px] text-white/40 mb-3">Quick self-check</div>
					<ul class="space-y-3 text-white/60 leading-relaxed">
						<li>Which degrees of freedom are currently binding for this Identity?</li>
						<li>Where does high activity meet low Mass under scarcity?</li>
						<li>What would change in allocation if the most binding scarce factor were relaxed?</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="px-8 py-16">
			<div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-6 text-white/55 leading-relaxed">
				<div class="rounded-2xl border border-cyan-400/20 bg-cyan-400/[0.04] p-6 text-base">
					<div class="mb-2 text-sm uppercase tracking-[3px] text-cyan-300/80">Related</div>
					<p>
						Next in Economics Single: Ownership → Preference → Opportunity (order under review).
						Physics parallel: Mass (density and stake) and the tension field.
						Biology parallel: Metabolism under finite energy and materials.
						Framework tension: Scarcity sharpens the tension between Identity force (sameness) and Intelligence force (future optionality).
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4 pt-4">
					<a href="/framework/ownership" class="px-6 py-3 rounded-full border border-white/20 text-white/70 hover:bg-white/5 transition">Ownership</a>
					<a href="/framework/preference" class="px-6 py-3 rounded-full border border-white/20 text-white/70 hover:bg-white/5 transition">Preference</a>
					<a href="/framework" class="px-6 py-3 rounded-full bg-white text-black font-medium hover:bg-white/90 transition">Framework</a>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>
	</main>
</Layout>
